## Why This Is Asked
Tests for real-time collaboration with CRDTs are complex due to concurrent edits and network churn. This question probes end-to-end orchestration, deterministic testing, and conflict-resolution validation.

## Key Concepts
- CRDT-based synchronization across two clients
- WebSocket messaging and presence propagation
- Offline edits and merge semantics
- Test determinism via mocked timelines and seeded data

## Code Example
```javascript
const { test, chromium } = require('@playwright/test');

test('collab doc CRDT merge with offline edits', async ({ browser }) => {
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();

  const pageA = await ctxA.newPage();
  const pageB = await ctxB.newPage();

  await pageA.goto('/doc/seeded-doc-id');
  await pageB.goto('/doc/seeded-doc-id');

  // Verify presence
  await expect(pageA.locator('.user-list')).toContainText('User B');
  await expect(pageB.locator('.user-list')).toContainText('User A');

  // Concurrent edits
  await pageA.locator('.editor').type('Hello ');
  await pageB.locator('.editor').type('World');

  // Verify CRDT merge (order may vary based on timestamps)
  await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText(/Hello World/);

  // Simulate disconnect
  await pageB.evaluate(() => window.mockWebSocket.close());
  await pageB.locator('.editor').type(' from offline');

  // Reconnect and verify merge
  await pageB.evaluate(() => window.mockWebSocket.reconnect());
  await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText(/Hello World from offline/);

  // Verify no duplicate edits
  const textA = await pageA.locator('.editor').textContent();
  const textB = await pageB.locator('.editor').textContent();
  expect(textA).toBe(textB);
});
```

**Answer: Playwright** — superior isolation with contexts, better mocking capabilities via browser automation, and more reliable for multi-tab/session scenarios.

